course: dictionary
lesson: 5989
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word mean can change its job depending on the words around it. Read the whole sentence first, then decide which meaning fits. «What does this word mean?» asks what a word stands for. «I mean the red one» tells you which one you are pointing to.
explain: Mean can also be about actions and feelings. «Don't be mean» means unkind. «I didn't mean to break it» means I did not plan to break it. The clue is the words around it: "to break it" shows an accident, not a plan.
explain: In maths, the mean is the average. To find it, add the numbers and divide by how many there are. When the sentence is about numbers, mean is a noun, not a rude word.
fact: Mean is a verb, an adjective and a noun, so its job changes with the sentence.
fact: «To mean» can mean to have a sense, to want to say something, or to plan to do something.
fact: «Mean» as an adjective means unkind: «Don't be mean.»
fact: In maths, the mean is the average: add the numbers, then divide by how many there are.
fact: If someone is mean to you again and again, tell an adult you trust.
exercise: «What does this word mean?» In this sentence, what does mean stand for? | To be unkind | *To have a sense | To plan something | The average
exercise: «I didn't mean to break it.» What does mean mean here? | *To plan or intend something | The average of two numbers | To be unkind | To say a word
exercise: «Don't be mean.» What does mean mean in this sentence? | Clever | *Unkind | Average | Tidy
test: In maths, what is the mean of a set of numbers? | *Their average, found by adding and dividing by how many there are | Their total | The number of numbers | The biggest number
test: «I mean the red one.» What does mean do in this sentence? | It shows I am planning something | It means unkind | It is the average of the colours | *It tells you which one I am talking about
exam: Which sentence uses mean as unkind? | «The mean of 2, 4 and 6 is 4.» | «What does this word mean?» | «I mean the blue hat.» | *«Don't be mean to your little brother.»
